Date: Tue, 5 Jan 93 12:12:54 PST
From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: Extropian filter

Kieth Henson writes:
>It is possible that the laws of the universe constrains the course of 
>technology development leading up to the ability to mess visually with 
>galaxies so that we *must* find "the rabbit hole."  The "rabbit hole" 
>is so attractive that *all* civilizations forsake "this" universe in 
>favor of a much more attractive one.  I used to envision this as a 
>hole into some other dimension, where, for example, the speed of light 
>was much faster.  Virtual reality might be such a hole. 

To explain the data, not only would all civilizations have to find
this whole, but everyone in such (immensely huge) civilizations would
have to go along.  If just a small percentage of contrarians decided
to conquer the stars, fashion be damned, the stars would get
conquered.  You might as well argue that the ocean bottoms will never
be colonized, because humans are so fond of land and sky, and there's
not much interesting down there anyway.

Both Keith and Mike Wiik suggest that small/fast uploaded creatures
might just lose interest in the galaxy.  

Keith writes:
>*nothing* which cannot be subjectively "accomplished" by uploaded 
>individuals in VR easier than in the real world.  ...
>If the efficiency of computation went up faster than the growth of a 
>civilization, it might be a *long* time before they felt pushed to try 
>for another star. ... If they had sped up their thinking, the stars 
>recede in inverse ratio.  

Mike writes:
>One ST:TNG style starship might more economically
>be replaced by trillions of nano-scale ships carrying (or "being") our
>posthuman descendents. Any large-scale galactic engineering might only
>be done by advanced intelligent species who had not gone thru singularity.
>... In _Marooned in Real Time_, the bulk of the population had passed thru
>singularity, yet remained invisible to the technically advanced people
>remaining.

I think Vinge explicitly described this as an implausible trick
that allowed him to write about something that would otherwise be
beyond comprehension.

I see no reason why the structures creatures build should be limited
by the size of their bodies, nor why their period of expansion should be
bounded by the subjective experience of one such creature.  

I'd say the key factor is the rate of growth of the economy.  If we
upload and think faster, then the absolute growth rate will increase,
even if the subjective growth rate (related to the interest rate per
subjective year) is less.  In absolute terms this should speed the day
when growth pressures encourage some moving outward.  

Currently a city is much larger than any one person, but is visibly
structured on the scale of the city.  A few engineering projects, like
damns or canals or railroad tracks, make structures on much larger
scales.  Eventually we hope to structure the planet, the solar system,
the galaxy, and more.  Even if we're all the size of dust grains
thinking a billion times faster, there will be *many* of us, requiring
larger scale physical structures to coordinate our interaction.  

Even if abstract information becomes the dominant thing of value, raw
material, space, and negentropy will not lose their value, and
expansion can harvest these now-idle resources.

