Amara Graps writes:
>I'm so surprised by these statements. HOW WOULD YOU KNOW if there
>were intelligent life in other galaxies and what exactly do you
>mean by "restructuring"?  I think that Robin is engaging in some
>serious hand-waving to think that we could detect life in other
>galaxies (even restructured ones) with our current technology.
>
>Yes, I know, Robin's response to me would be similar to Ray's and 
>Hal's: 
>
>>You're not thinking big enough!  I was imagining a civilization
>>significantly beyond a Dyson sphere stage...
>
>So, since I'm not thinking big enough, let me bring you back down
>to earth, and demonstrate to you how difficult it is to observe
>intelligent life elsewhere. 

Amara continues with a long and informative review of IR detection of
other solar systems, and then concludes with:

>Stars with planets are difficult enough to detect as described above,
>even though there may be many out there. What sort of restructuring
>could be done to galaxies to make it resolvable to infrared
>astronomers? The restructuring would have to be done so that a large
>region that's spatially resolvable from the galactic center is
>radiating brightly in the infrared. The surface area and the
>temperature are the important pieces for the infrared astronomers. And
>then to detect "intelligent" life, the restructuring has to be done in
>a way that makes it appear "unnatural." 

I will defer to Amara's expertize in IR astronomy.  But we should be
able to agree that our current data excludes some possible galactic
restructuring, and as we get more data more will be excluded.  Surely
*our* galaxy has not been restructured, meaning having large scale
(>1Kpc) clearly geometric features radiating at some temperature >
background.  And I have seen IR maps of some other galaxies with
significant spatial resolution - so those have not been restructured
at that scale and temperature.  I would be fascinated to read a review
giving graphs of excluded regions of temperature/scale of structures,
for a few nearby galaxies, and also for classes of more distant
galaxies.

My point was that even the little data we have now speaks volumes if
we'll listen to it - it constrains the rate at which planets evolve
civilizations which restructure galaxies.  Every new factor of ten we
get in excluding ten times more galaxies, or at ten times more
temperatures, or ten times more scales, is only a small correction on
the (already known to be rather large) extropian filter.  No need to
wait a few decades before we start to draw conclusions.

Robin Hanson

