Date: Tue, 5 Jan 93 14:30:12 EST
From: pmetzger@shearson.com (Perry E. Metzger)
Subject: Probabilities and Aliens

A few people have posted messages on the alien civilizations thread that
use probabilities rather loosely. I think it would be useful to point out
that virtually all the calculations anyone has done in this thread, as well
as Frank Drake's famous equation, all assume, I believe very eroneously,
that the probabilities involved are independant. Since I think that it is
highly plausible that technological civilizations tend to preclude the growth
of other intelligences, I would say this is an important consideration.
Remember that most standard probability theory completely breaks down when
the independance assumption is lost.

On a similar note, many people have noted things like "well, I don't see
why you would assume aliens would send out Von Neuman Machines".

Lets do a gedanken experiment, shall we? All humans don't have the same
motivations, and there are a lot of us. Although most sane people wouldn't
produce computer viruses, for instance, some people will. With advanced
nanotech, a 20 year old college student could build a spacefaring Von
Neuman machine. What are the odds that no one, absolutely no one at all,
in the entire human race, mind you, would build such a machine if virtually
anyone could do it?

Similarly, what are the odds that no member of any alien race, with alien
races being fairly common, would have ever built a single Von Neuman machine?
Given that with one Von Neuman machine released one soon will have infected
every star system in all directions with the "wave front" traveling out
at an appreciable fraction of the speed of light, the odds that there is
a high tech civilization out there anywhere in our light cone with advanced
nanotech seem much lower. Remember, even if all but one of the members of
that civilization become navel starers, it takes just one building such a
device for us to have some evidence of them. I bet there would be a lot more
than one such individual, so the situation is even stranger than it would
seem on the surface.

Perry

