Robin Hanson has started a great thread with his provocative
"extropian filter"!  Keith Henson suggests that VR may be a "rabbit 
hole", a path that is universally far more attractive than the 
macroengineering of galaxies.  This may be true if there is no lower 
limit to the scale of the universe.  Call it "boundless implosion".  
If such a limit is reached, expansion once again becomes attractive.

A macroengineering answer to the Fermi Paradox is that our solar system 
sits in a giant planetarium.  All the interesting stuff Amara has seen
is just a show, a magnificent 3D encryption.  If the planetarium's 
computer keeps improving its capability faster than we are improving
our observational capability, we will never catch up and detect the ruse.  
Such interstellar camoflauge might be an emergent property of economics
or warfare rather than purposefully targeted at humans, though I cannot 
suggest how this would come about.

Dani Eder pointed out that the subjective time of digital interstellar 
travel is zero, which has interesting consequences.  If a digital being
travels to Mars and back, it could lose quadrillions of computer cycles 
against its competitors back on Earth.  Going to Alpha Centauri, it
might get hopelessly behind.   This works in reverse; a more compact
computer has faster cycles.  This provides a strong economic motivation
for implosion, thus a "rabbit hole" more attractive than macroengineering.

Perhaps a few uploaders don't care much about economic power --
I deal with the problem of motivation below -- and decide to travel.
The digital being can travel any distance and any amount of time 
into the future, in zero subjective time.  If it galaxy-hops 
every few subjective seconds, it might soon find itself faced with the
entropic death of the universe!  Such a trip would be subjectively
far faster, easier and more interesting than macroengineering 
a galaxy, but it is a one-way trip.

Would imploding civilizations be swamped by expanding civilizations?
Computational compactness brings not only economic but also military
power, so imploders could overcome any invaders.  This is even more
likely if they preceeded the invaders to uploading by the suggested 
mean time of 1,000 years.

Perhaps then, we have this filter against macroengineering: most beings 
implode, gaining ever more economic superiority through faster cycles.  The
few who choose to leave the nest to see the universe, are either destroyed 
by the superior imploders or find themselves at the entropic death end of 
the universe.  I wouldn't call this an extropian filter though, except
for the entropic universe death part.

A second filter, a potent filter against any form of extropian universe,
involves motivation.  Self-genocide may be a universal trait of sentients 
with conscious control over their own bodily functions.  Demographics 
indicate that self-genocide is the fate of large-scale, independent, 
hi-tech human colonization efforts under the cultural assumptions common 
to all developed countries. (I demonstrated this in detail in a series 
of posts a few months ago).  Any high-tech culture must rely on immigration 
from a primitive culture or dwindle away to extinction.  For humans, the 
self-genocide is achieved simply by effective birth control, and can only 
be delayed a few thousand years by non-uploading methods of longevity.  
The anti-natal memes symbiotic to birth control evolve far faster and can 
dominate pro-natal genetic response all the way to the point of extinction.  
Unless there exists a more fertile pro-natal meme -- none has been 
demonstated, and there is no theoretical reason to expect one to prevail -- 
we are destined to genocide ourselves.

Some sentient beings may avoid this fate by having evolved a 
biological substrate immune to such major efforts at memetic control,
up to the point where uploading is achieved.  For any sentient being, 
motivation to reproduce and to continue living may be tied to their 
biological substrate, and necessarily destroyed in the process of 
uploading.  Thus, both boundless expansion and immortality would be out 
of reach, since the biological substrates cannot be backed up and would 
eventually be destroyed by accident.  The problem of joining extropian 
motivations with sufficiently advanced intelligence might turn out to 
be computationally intractable, or vastly improbable as an emergent
property of the universe. 

